l bytes inventoried and available; no new semantic reconstruction claimed by copying/display","claimBoundary":"Received diagrams and annotations preserve claims to examine; their contents are not adopted as mathematical or historical proof."},{"id":"provided-image:2899716c381d3f2e8ca84e14225e6c7b4d801ed94246bf29705ad24505e7b3c1","url":"assets/figures/2899716c381d3f2e8ca84e14225e6c7b4d801ed94246bf29705ad24505e7b3c1.png","sha256":"2899716c381d3f2e8ca84e14225e6c7b4d801ed94246bf29705ad24505e7b3c1","occurrences":[{"name":"Analysis output 1.png","path":"C:/Users/Brend/Downloads/Analysis output 1.png","bytes":886045,"sha256":"2899716c381d3f2e8ca84e14225e6c7b4d801ed94246bf29705ad24505e7b3c1"}],"processingStatus":"Original bytes inventoried and available; no new semantic reconstruction claimed by copying/display","claimBoundary":"Received diagrams and annotations preserve claims to examine; their contents are not adopted as mathematical or historical proof."}],"unavailableFigures":[],"symbolCensus":{"dated-witnesses":{"early_hebrew":{"object":"Arad ostracon 16, Elyashiv archive, Judah, ca. 600 BCE","locator":"verso line 1, first word","edition_token":"אם","transliteration":"ʾm","edition":"https://www.tau.ac.il/~ashaus/2017_BASOR_t.pdf","metadata":"https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/a-brand-new-old-inscription-arad-ostracon-16-rediscovered-via-mul/","image":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0178400.g004","translation_figure":"arad16-fig6.png","translation_figure_sha256":"34fad1f2ac5d8115e6925becfad19b59bfeafddef7b078572e406b3a1dc1a32f","credit":"Faigenbaum-Golovin et al. (2017), PLOS ONE, DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0178400, CC BY; Fig. 6 re-encoded as PNG. Fig. 6 is the translation, Fig. 4 the object image."},"imperial_aramaic":{"object":"Berlin Pap. Ber. P. 13464, TAD A.4.01 / Cowley 21, Elephantine; Darius year 5, conventionally 419 BCE","locator":"recto line 01, unbracketed form rendered your brother immediately before personal name","edition_token":"אחוכמ","edition":"https://deaps.osu.edu/text_objects/12277","museum":"https://elephantine.smb.museum/objects/object.php?o=100463","edition_author":"James D. Moore","source_note":"The opening address of this same line is partly restored. Selected a later unbracketed word in Moore's transcription. Museum direct request returned 500; museum ID corroborated by indexed record."},"comparison":"Both editions recognize an initial aleph. Modern square-script rendering and scholarly transliteration describe a letter identification, not a date-specific observed phone. Both witnesses are written documents; neither measures attack, pitch, phase, timbre or sonic intention. Different genres (supplies vs festival letter); do not infer identical vocal performance. No blind evidence: both inscriptions were selected after searching for aleph.","supplements":["docs/evidence/latin-vindolanda-291.json","docs/evidence/english-a-pronunciation-control.json"]},"reading-tradition-prior-art":{"author":"Benjamin Paul Kantor","title":"The Linguistic Classification of the Reading Traditions of Biblical Hebrew: A Phyla-and-Waves Model","publicationDate":"2023-08-30","source":"https://www.openbookpublishers.com/books/10.11647/obp.0210","candidateReuse":{"chapter2":"Methodology, pp.5-19","chapter4":"Shared innovations, pp.60-144","chapter5":"Influence, contact and convergence, pp.145-180","chapter6":"Relationships of reading traditions, pp.181-185"},"scope":"Publisher description establishes a prior-art method for relating Hebrew reading traditions. Chapter-level method and individual claims have not yet been audited here.","sunnyQuestion":"Can shared innovations and contact separately constrain genealogical edges among source-native symbol-sound systems?","acceptance":"Recover explicit characters and shared innovations, reconstruct the author derivations, then assess transfer conditions before applying them to SUNNY geometry."},"alef-operational-hypotheses":{"question":"What phonetic operation does a dot on aleph encode within each witnessed reading practice?","hypotheses":[{"id":"articulation","scope":"Medial canonical aleph in standard Tiberian reading","claim":"The dot requires consonantal realization but does not itself prescribe gemination.","prediction":"Articulation instructions should suffice; a diagnostic gemination mark in an aligned transcription needs another explanation.","test":"Collate Or2552 Job33:21 and controls establishing whether its shadda marks single consonants."},{"id":"gemination","scope":"Medial canonical aleph in standard Tiberian reading","claim":"The dot prescribes a geminate realization.","prediction":"An independent transcription system that marks gemination should mark this locus accordingly.","test":"Or2552 supplies a reported supporting instance; reproduce the sign and the scribal control distribution."},{"id":"universal-consonantal-dot","scope":"All manuscripts and word positions","claim":"Every dot on aleph requires consonantal realization.","prediction":"No dotted aleph may be nonconsonantal.","test":"Khan p148 reports NS248.2 counterexamples. Verify marks and the argument for nonconsonantal reading before independent exclusion."}],"dependencies":["job33-alef-gemination-control.json","tiberian-alef-testimony.json","wlc-dotted-alef-census.json"],"sharedConsequences":["A geminate consonant also has consonantal realization. Detecting closure alone cannot distinguish the first two hypotheses.","No exact doubling ratio follows from the label geminate. Speech rate, context and realization require their own evidence."],"nextEvidence":["Or2552 target plus independently classified shadda controls","NS248.2 final aleph marks and reading context"],"limits":"These are explicit competing operational hypotheses. The dot inventory is orthographic evidence. Original sacred intention, exact waveform, and transfer to earlier scripts remain unresolved."},"secunda-transcription-control":{"source":"https://www.lib.cam.ac.uk/collections/departments/taylor-schechter-genizah-research-unit/fragment-month/fotm-2019/fragment-1","sha256":"a06dbe581becf9b6613cbc68987572c9393b838de27c45cd2e79e90643ff60bc","shelfmark":"T-S 12.182","editionEnding":"αυεω","letterUnderDiscussion":"ayin","sourceInterpretations":["preservation of syllable structure","approximation of a guttural articulation"],"letterByLetterPhysicalVerification":"pending","use":"Control for ambiguity in reverse inference from Greek spelling. No acoustic frequency or aleph reading supplied."},"secunda-ps46-control":{"source":"https://books.openbookpublishers.com/10.11647/obp.0210.03.pdf","locator":"Kantor, chapter 3, table 3, Ps 46:1-2. Hebrew column reconstructed in brackets; word-level rows split at printed separators.","pairs":[["על","αλ"],["עלמות","αλμωθ"],["עזר","εζρ"],["אלהים","ελωειμ"]],"question":"Do Hebrew word-initial aleph and ayin have distinct initial Greek vowel letters in these examples?","consequence":"Initial epsilon is compatible with both aleph and ayin, and initial ayin is compatible with alpha and epsilon. These word-initial examples do not identify the function of the internal epsilon in Ps 22:25.","exposure":"Selected from an inspected publication. No independent blind evaluation. Greek initials are orthographic observations; no isolated consonant pronunciation supplied."},"secunda-internal-vowel-controls":{"source":"https://books.openbookpublishers.com/10.11647/obp.0210.03.pdf","edition":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openscriptures/morphhb/3d15126fb1ef74867fc1434be1942e837932691f/wlc/Ps.xml","examples":[{"verse":"Ps.35.19","word":"יִקְרְצוּ","consonants":"יקרצו","GreekPrinted":"ϊκερσου"},{"verse":"Ps.35.24","word":"יִשְׂמְחוּ","consonants":"ישמחו","GreekPrinted":"ιεσεμου"}],"finding":"Printed p25 supplies Greek forms for both previously pinned Hebrew examples; author analyzes insertion before a sonorant. These examples lack ayin in their written roots. Their grammatical and phonotactic environment differs from Psalm22:25.","exposure":"Autho